From 9598f9811985d63c0546cdc4a3e0f6378184a211 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 19:48:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] offers: mint, list and decode BOLT 12 offers

A new offers package and an offersrpc sub-server (build tag offersrpc,
in the release and testing tags) let the node mint offers for its own
chain, list, disable and enable them, and decode any BOLT 12 string.
The surface is lncli offer create|list|disable|enable|decode and REST
under /v2/offers.

Every offer names this chain in offer_chains and the node's identity
key in offer_issuer_id. The chain is pinned on the write side too:
bolt12/offers_api.go, the one file in bolt12/ that is not from LND
master, wraps the upstream writer validators so a message that names
no chain, which BOLT 12 reads as Bitcoin mainnet, is refused before it
is encoded. A test pins the Bitcoin genesis the default falls back to.

offer_metadata is deterministic: the first 16 bytes of an HMAC over the
offer's other fields under a secret derived from the wallet's base
encryption key, the same key the channel backup key comes from. So the
same fields always mint the same offer, a miner who restores from seed
mints the string the pool already holds again, and the node recognises
its own offers from their fields alone. The secret the offer's blinded
paths carry as path_id derives the same way, from the fields without
metadata and paths, so a request that mirrors the offer is enough to
check it. Blinded paths themselves arrive with the onion-message step;
a PathBuilder hook and the announced-address gate are in place.

Offers live in a kvdb bucket on the channel state DB as TLV records
that tolerate unknown types; a damaged record is skipped by List and
still reported by Get. quantity_max is a hard maximum and quantity_any
sets the spec's zero. Decoding reports for_this_chain from the chain
fields alone, with valid, validation_error and ours alongside. Input
errors are typed and map to InvalidArgument; the offer cap maps to
ResourceExhausted. Macaroons reuse the invoices entity.

Falafel's JSON stub for the package has to exist before the generator
runs (it formats the file before protoc writes it), so an empty one is
committed and regenerated with the rest.

(cherry picked from commit e24fc5b668bd0bb7b518728956209fe7dba636a9)
